tags: germany

= Artists and Intellectuals

Revolution is fashionable in the cafés of Berlin. George Grosz draws generals and profiteers with pigs' faces, and John Heartfield and Wieland Herzfelde print them at the Malik-Verlag. [? if year >= 1920 : Erwin Piscator has opened a Proletarian Theatre in the workers' halls. ?] Some comrades think the artists will be useful. Others think they are tourists.

- @welcome: Take their money.
- @posters: Put them to work on the posters.
- @easy_discard

@welcome
subtitle: 2 funds from the comfortable. The workers in the districts are suspicious.
on-arrival: {!
Q.resources += 2;
for (var d of Q.districts) {
    Q['district_' + d + '_loyalty'] -= 2;
}
!}

A gallery owner on the Kurfürstendamm holds an auction for the party. The workers' delegates who are invited do not know where to put their caps.

@posters
subtitle: Costs 1 fund. Berlin is papered with them. Worker support rises.
choose-if: resources >= 1
unavailable-subtitle: We have no funds.
on-arrival: {!
Q.resources -= 1;
Q.worker_support += 4;
RG.district('berlin', {militancy: 3});
!}

Heartfield's montages appear on every hoarding in Berlin. The police tear them down in the morning, and by evening there are new ones.
